---
type: Component Reference
title: Capture Extension
description: Browser extension (Manifest V3) for capturing X/Twitter posts from the DOM
tags: [capture, extension, browser-extension, typescript, manifest-v3, twitter]
---

# Capture Extension

The browser extension is the user-facing capture interface. It runs on X/Twitter pages, extracts post data from the DOM, and sends it to the local capture server.

## Project Structure

```
capture/extension/
├── src/
│   ├── background.ts          # Service worker entry point
│   ├── contentScript.ts       # DOM extraction on twitter.com/x.com
│   ├── types.ts               # Shared TypeScript interfaces
│   ├── popup/                 # React popup UI
│   │   ├── App.tsx
│   │   ├── CaptureForm.tsx
│   │   ├── Settings.tsx
│   │   └── styles.css
│   └── utils/
│       ├── dom.ts             # DOM selectors and extraction helpers
│       ├── api.ts             # Server communication
│       └── storage.ts         # Chrome storage wrapper
├── public/
│   └── manifest.json          # Manifest V3 manifest
├── webpack.config.js          # or vite.config.ts
├── package.json
└── tsconfig.json
```

## Manifest V3 Configuration

```json
{
  "manifest_version": 3,
  "name": "Capture",
  "version": "0.1.0",
  "description": "Save X/Twitter posts locally as you browse",
  "permissions": ["storage", "activeTab", "scripting"],
  "host_permissions": [
    "https://twitter.com/*",
    "https://x.com/*"
  ],
  "background": {
    "service_worker": "background.js",
    "type": "module"
  },
  "content_scripts": [{
    "matches": ["https://twitter.com/*", "https://x.com/*"],
    "js": ["contentScript.js"],
    "run_at": "document_idle"
  }],
  "action": {
    "default_popup": "popup.html",
    "default_title": "Capture"
  },
  "web_accessible_resources": [{
    "resources": ["popup.html", "popup.js", "styles.css"],
    "matches": ["https://twitter.com/*", "https://x.com/*"]
  }]
}
```

## Content Script (`contentScript.ts`)

Runs in the context of twitter.com/x.com pages. Extracts tweet data from the DOM.

### Extraction Strategy

The content script uses multiple selector strategies to handle DOM changes:

```typescript
// Primary selectors (data-testid based, more stable)
const SELECTORS = {
  tweetArticle: 'article[data-testid="tweet"]',
  tweetText: '[data-testid="tweetText"]',
  authorLink: '[data-testid="User-Name"] a[href^="/"]',
  timestamp: 'time[datetime]',
  links: 'a[href^="http"]:not([href*="twitter.com"]):not([href*="x.com"])',
  media: '[data-testid="tweetPhoto"], [data-testid="videoPlayer"]'
};

// Fallback selectors (class-based, for legacy twitter.com)
const LEGACY_SELECTORS = {
  tweetArticle: '.tweet, .js-stream-tweet',
  tweetText: '.tweet-text, .js-tweet-text',
  // ...
};
```

### Extracted Data Structure

```typescript
interface ExtractedTweet {
  url: string;                    // https://x.com/user/status/123456789
  authorHandle: string;           // @username
  authorDisplayName: string;      // User Name
  content: string;                // Full tweet text
  timestamp: string;              // ISO 8601 from <time datetime>
  links: string[];                // External URLs in tweet
  mediaUrls: string[];            // Image/video URLs (if any)
  conversationId?: string;        // For thread detection
  inReplyToStatusId?: string;     // If reply
}
```

### Message Passing

```typescript
// Content script → Background
chrome.runtime.sendMessage({
  type: 'CAPTURE_REQUEST',
  payload: extractedTweet
});

// Background → Content script (response)
{ type: 'CAPTURE_RESPONSE', success: boolean, captureId?: string, error?: string }
```

## Background Service Worker (`background.ts`)

Handles API communication, settings, and offline queue.

### Responsibilities

1. **Server Communication**: POST captures to `http://localhost:8765/capture`
2. **Settings Management**: Server URL, auto-capture preferences
3. **Offline Queue**: IndexedDB queue for captures when server unreachable
4. **Retry Logic**: Exponential backoff, max 3 retries
5. **Context Menu**: Optional right-click "Capture this post"

### API Client

```typescript
async function sendCapture(payload: CapturePayload): Promise<CaptureResponse> {
  const serverUrl = await getSetting('serverUrl') || 'http://localhost:8765';

  const response = await fetch(`${serverUrl}/capture`, {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify(payload)
  });

  if (!response.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Server error: ${response.status}`);
  }

  return response.json();
}
```

### Offline Queue (IndexedDB)

```typescript
// Queue structure
interface QueuedCapture {
  id: string;           // UUID
  payload: CapturePayload;
  attempts: number;
  queuedAt: string;     // ISO 8601
}

// On startup: process queue
async function processQueue() {
  const queue = await getQueue();
  for (const item of queue) {
    try {
      await sendCapture(item.payload);
      await removeFromQueue(item.id);
    } catch (e) {
      if (item.attempts >= 3) {
        await moveToDeadLetter(item);
      } else {
        await incrementAttempts(item.id);
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Popup UI (`popup/`)

React-based UI for capture confirmation and notes.

### Components

| Component | Purpose |
|-----------|---------|
| `App.tsx` | Root, handles capture flow state |
| `CaptureForm.tsx` | Shows extracted data, notes input, save button |
| `Settings.tsx` | Server URL, preferences |
| `styles.css` | Minimal styling, matches Twitter aesthetic |

### Capture Flow

```
1. User clicks extension icon
         │
         ▼
2. Popup opens, requests current tab's tweet data from content script
         │
         ▼
3. Content script extracts and returns tweet data
         │
         ▼
4. CaptureForm displays: author, content, links, [notes textarea]
         │
         ▼
5. User optionally adds notes, clicks "Save"
         │
         ▼
6. Background sends to server, shows success/error toast
         │
         ▼
7. Popup closes auto after 2s on success
```

## Shared Types (`types.ts`)

```typescript
// Payload sent to server
interface CapturePayload {
  platform: 'twitter';
  url: string;
  author: {
    handle: string;
    displayName: string;
  };
  content: string;
  links: string[];
  mediaUrls?: string[];
  metadata: {
    capturedAt: string;        // ISO 8601
    extensionVersion: string;
    conversationId?: string;
    inReplyToStatusId?: string;
  };
  notes?: string;              // User notes from popup
}

// Server response
interface CaptureResponse {
  success: boolean;
  id?: string;                 // UUID assigned by server
  error?: string;
}

// Extension settings
interface ExtensionSettings {
  serverUrl: string;           // Default: http://localhost:8765
  autoCapture: boolean;        // Capture without popup confirmation
  showNotifications: boolean;
}
```

## Build & Development

```bash
# Install dependencies
cd capture/extension && npm install

# Development (watch mode)
npm run dev

# Production build
npm run build
# Output: dist/ (loadable extension)
```

### Build Output

```
dist/
├── manifest.json
├── background.js
├── contentScript.js
├── popup.html
├── popup.js
├── styles.css
└── assets/               # Icons, fonts
```

## Testing

```bash
# Unit tests (Jest + ts-jest)
npm test

# E2E tests (Playwright)
npm run test:e2e
```

### Key Test Scenarios

- Content script extracts correctly from twitter.com and x.com DOM
- Background handles server offline → queues → retries on reconnect
- Popup displays extracted data, accepts notes, submits successfully
- Settings persist across browser restarts
- Content script handles deleted/removed tweets gracefully

## Debugging

```bash
# Background worker console
chrome://extensions → Capture → "Inspect views: Service worker"

# Content script console
DevTools on twitter.com → Console (filter by "Capture")

# Popup console
Right-click extension icon → "Inspect popup"
```

## Common Issues

| Issue | Cause | Fix |
|-------|-------|-----|
| No tweets extracted | DOM selectors outdated | Update selectors in `utils/dom.ts` |
| Server connection refused | Server not running | Start `python server.py` in `capture/server/` |
| CORS error | Server CORS config | Ensure server allows `chrome-extension://<id>` |
| Popup blank | Build error | Check `npm run build` output, rebuild |

## Future Enhancements

- **Keyboard shortcut**: `Alt+S` to quick-capture without popup
- **Thread capture**: Detect and capture entire conversation threads
- **Media download**: Optionally save images/videos locally
- **Multi-profile**: Support multiple server endpoints (work/personal)

// Content script for Capture. Runs on x.com / twitter.com.
//
// Shows a single "Capture" pill when hovering a tweet OR a comment. Because a
// comment is itself a tweet, the same pills/scraping is reused down the tree:
//   - No capture open  → clicking the pill captures the root tweet and opens
//     the side panel (a capture in progress begins).
//   - Capture open     → hovering comments shows the same pill ("Add"/"Remove");
//     clicking adds the comment (nested under its DOM parent) or removes it.
//     Hovering an unrelated tweet starts a fresh capture (replaces).
//
// The side panel is the live view of the open capture; every change is pushed
// to the background worker (which holds state across navigation) and the
// panel re-renders.

(() => {
  "use strict";

  const PILL_ID = "capture-pill";
  const ADDED_ATTR = "data-capture-added";

  let currentEl = null; // the tweet/comment the cursor is over
  let pill = null;
  // Open capture state. { rootEl, rootNode, added: Map<Element, {node,parent,depth}> }
  let capture = null;

  // Inject a subtle highlight for curated comments (decision C).
  const styleEl = document.createElement("style");
  styleEl.textContent =
    `[${ADDED_ATTR}]{box-shadow:inset 3px 0 0 #1d9bf0;}`;
  document.documentElement.appendChild(styleEl);

  // Remove any leftover pill from a previous injection. After an extension
  // reload without a page refresh, the old page may still hold a stale
  // #capture-pill whose handlers were torn down — clicking it looked like
  // "nothing happens." Removing it here prevents dead-duplicate pills.
  const stale = document.getElementById(PILL_ID);
  if (stale) stale.remove();

  // --- Scraping -----------------------------------------------------------
  // Each helper returns "" / [] on failure rather than throwing, so a DOM
  // change degrades gracefully instead of breaking the whole capture.

  function scrapeTweetURL(tweetEl) {
    const a = tweetEl.querySelector('a[href*="/status/"]');
    if (!a) return "";
    const href = a.getAttribute("href") || "";
    const m = href.match(/\/[^/]+\/status\/\d+/);
    if (!m) return "";
    return "https://x.com" + m[0];
  }

  function scrapeAuthor(tweetEl) {
    const region = tweetEl.querySelector('[data-testid="User-Name"]');
    if (!region) return "";
    const a = region.querySelector("a");
    const href = a && a.getAttribute("href");
    const handle = href && href.length > 1 ? "@" + href.replace(/^\//, "") : "";
    const display = region.innerText || "";
    return handle + (display ? (handle ? " · " : "") + display.trim() : "");
  }

  function scrapeTweetText(tweetEl) {
    const node = tweetEl.querySelector('[data-testid="tweetText"]');
    if (!node) return "";
    return (node.innerText || node.textContent || "").trim();
  }

  function isInternalTweetLink(href) {
    return /^https?:\/\/([^/]*\.)?(x|twitter)\.com\//i.test(href);
  }

  // Trim a bare word that X glues onto a domain in the display text (e.g.
  // "explee.comAutoGTM" → "explee.com"). Keeps real paths intact
  // ("github.com/tahul/space-rabbit"). The TLD (last dot-label) should be 2-24
  // lowercase letters; anything word-char glued directly after it is junk.
  function trimGluedDomain(url) {
    const hadScheme = /^https?:\/\//i.test(url);
    const body = url.replace(/^https?:\/\//i, "");
    const pm = body.match(/^([^/?#:]*)([\/?#:].*)?$/);
    const domain = pm ? pm[1] : body;
    const path = pm && pm[2] ? pm[2] : "";
    if (!/\./.test(domain)) return url;
    const lastDot = domain.lastIndexOf(".");
    const pre = domain.slice(0, lastDot + 1);
    const tldAndGlue = domain.slice(lastDot + 1);
    // Case-sensitive so glued uppercase junk ("…comAutoGTM") is trimmed while
    // a normal lowercase TLD ("…com") is kept intact.
    const tldMatch = tldAndGlue.match(/^([a-z]{2,24})/);
    let cleanDomain = domain;
    if (tldMatch && tldMatch[0].length < tldAndGlue.length) {
      cleanDomain = pre + tldMatch[0];
    }
    return (hadScheme ? "https://" : "") + cleanDomain + path;
  }

  // Resolve the real destination of a link anchor. X wraps external links in
  // t.co shortlinks but renders the true destination as the anchor's title and
  // display text, so we recover that instead of keeping the opaque shortlink.
  function resolveLink(a) {
    const href = a.href || "";
    if (/^https?:\/\/t\.co\//i.test(href)) {
      // 1) Prefer the title attribute — X sets it to the real destination URL.
      const title = (a.getAttribute("title") || "").trim();
      if (/^https?:\/\/[^\s]+$/i.test(title)) {
        return title.replace(/[),.'"\u201d\u2019]+$/, "");
      }
      // 2) Recover from the display text (whitespace-tokenized, glued-word-safe).
      const text = (a.textContent || "").replace(/\s+/g, " ").trim();
      const token =
        (text.match(
          /(?:https?:\/\/)?[\w-]+(?:\.[\w-]+)+(?::\d+)?(?:[\/?#][^\s…]*)?/
        ) || [""])[0];
      if (token && token.length > 4) {
        const cleaned = trimGluedDomain(token);
        return /^https?:\/\//i.test(cleaned)
          ? cleaned
          : "https://" + cleaned;
      }
    }
    // 3) Fallback: the raw href, minus tracking params (e.g. ?twclid=…).
    return href.replace(/([?#].*)$/, "");
  }

  function scrapeLinks(tweetEl, currentTweetUrl) {
    const links = [];
    const seen = new Set();
    for (const a of tweetEl.querySelectorAll('a[href^="http"]')) {
      const href = a.href || "";

      const statusMatch = href.match(/\/([^\/]+)\/status\/(\d+)/);
      if (statusMatch) {
        const statusUrl = "https://x.com/" + statusMatch[1] + "/status/" + statusMatch[2];
        if (statusUrl !== currentTweetUrl && !seen.has(statusUrl)) {
          seen.add(statusUrl);
          links.push(statusUrl);
        }
        continue;
      }

      if (isInternalTweetLink(href)) continue;
      const resolved = resolveLink(a);
      if (!resolved) continue;
      const norm = resolved.replace(/[),.'"\u201d]+$/, "");
      if (seen.has(norm)) continue;
      seen.add(norm);
      links.push(norm);
    }
    return links;
  }

  // The recursive node scraper. A comment is scraped exactly like a tweet;
  // the only difference is parent_url and its position in the tree.
  function scrapeNode(tweetEl, parentUrl) {
    const tweet_url = scrapeTweetURL(tweetEl);
    return {
      tweet_url,
      author: scrapeAuthor(tweetEl),
      tweet_text: scrapeTweetText(tweetEl),
      links: scrapeLinks(tweetEl, tweet_url),
      parent_url: parentUrl || null,
      notes: "",
      children: [],
    };
  }

  // --- Capture state machine ---------------------------------------------
  function startCapture(el) {
    capture = {
      rootEl: el,
      rootNode: scrapeNode(el, null),
      added: new Map(),
    };
    console.log("Capture: starting capture on", capture.rootNode.tweet_url);
    sendCapture(true); // first message: open the panel
  }

  function sendCapture(openPanel) {
    const msg = openPanel ? "capture-scraped" : "update-pending-capture";
    chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ type: msg, data: capture.rootNode }, (resp) => {
      if (chrome.runtime.lastError) {
        console.error("Capture: background not available", chrome.runtime.lastError);
      }
    });
  }

  // The reply thread container on a tweet's detail page. Replies render here;
  // the root tweet usually does not. Used to decide whether a hovered element
  // is a comment of the current capture or something unrelated.
  function conversationThreadFor(rootEl) {
    const page = rootEl.closest('[data-testid="primaryColumn"]');
    if (!page || !rootEl.isConnected) return null;
    return page.querySelector('[data-testid="conversationThread"]');
  }

  function classifyHover(el) {
    if (!capture) return "root"; // nothing open → any tweet can become a root
    const rootEl = capture.rootEl;
    if (el === rootEl) return "root";
    if (!rootEl.isConnected) return "unrelated";
    const thread = conversationThreadFor(rootEl);
    if (thread && thread.contains(el)) return "comment";
    // Fallback: if we're on a status/detail page and the hovered element sits
    // in the same primary column as the root (but isn't the root), treat it as
    // a reply. X's reply-testid isn't stable, so the URL is a reliable hint
    // that we're inside a conversation.
    const page = rootEl.closest('[data-testid="primaryColumn"]');
    const elPage = el.closest('[data-testid="primaryColumn"]');
    if (page && elPage === page && isOnDetailPage()) return "comment";
    return "unrelated"; // e.g. a timeline tweet → start a new capture
  }

  function isOnDetailPage() {
    return /\/status\/\d+/.test(window.location.pathname);
  }

  function findNearestCapturedAncestor(el) {
    let cur = el.parentElement;
    while (cur) {
      if (cur === capture.rootEl) return { parent: capture.rootNode, depth: 0 };
      const entry = capture.added.get(cur);
      if (entry) return { parent: entry.node, depth: entry.depth };
      cur = cur.parentElement;
    }
    return { parent: capture.rootNode, depth: 0 };
  }

  function addComment(el) {
    const { parent, depth } = findNearestCapturedAncestor(el);
    const node = scrapeNode(el, parent.tweet_url);
    parent.children.push(node);
    capture.added.set(el, { node, parent, depth: depth + 1 });
    el.setAttribute(ADDED_ATTR, "true");
    sendCapture(false); // live update, panel already open
  }

  function removeNodeFromParent(parent, node) {
    const idx = parent.children.indexOf(node);
    if (idx >= 0) parent.children.splice(idx, 1);
  }

  // Removing a comment also removes its whole subtree (children can't be
  // orphans under a removed parent).
  function unmarkSubtree(el, node) {
    el.removeAttribute(ADDED_ATTR);
    for (const childNode of node.children) {
      for (const [childEl, childEntry] of capture.added) {
        if (childEntry.node === childNode) {
          unmarkSubtree(childEl, childNode);
          capture.added.delete(childEl);
          break;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  function removeComment(el) {
    const entry = capture.added.get(el);
    if (!entry) return;
    unmarkSubtree(el, entry.node);
    removeNodeFromParent(entry.parent, entry.node);
    capture.added.delete(el);
    sendCapture(false);
  }

  function onCaptureClick() {
    if (!currentEl) return;
    const el = currentEl;

    if (!capture) {
      startCapture(el);
      hidePill();
      return;
    }

    const kind = classifyHover(el);
    if (kind === "root") {
      // Inert — this capture's root is already in progress.
      return;
    }
    if (kind === "comment") {
      if (capture.added.has(el)) removeComment(el);
      else addComment(el);
      hidePill();
    } else {
      // Unrelated tweet → replace the open capture.
      startCapture(el);
      hidePill();
    }
  }

  // --- Capture pill -------------------------------------------------------
  function ensurePill() {
    if (pill) return pill;
    pill = document.createElement("div");
    pill.id = PILL_ID;
    pill.setAttribute("role", "button");
    pill.style.cssText = [
      "position:fixed",
      "z-index:2147483647",
      "padding:7px 14px",
      "border-radius:9999px",
      "background:#1d9bf0",
      "color:#fff",
      "font:600 13px/1 system-ui,sans-serif",
      "white-space:nowrap",
      "cursor:pointer",
      "box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.35)",
      "pointer-events:auto",
      "user-select:none",
      "display:none",
    ].join(";");
    pill.addEventListener("mousedown", (e) => e.preventDefault());
    pill.addEventListener("click", onCaptureClick);
    document.documentElement.appendChild(pill);
    return pill;
  }

  function pillLabel(el) {
    if (!capture) return "Capture";
    const kind = classifyHover(el);
    if (kind === "comment") return capture.added.has(el) ? "Remove" : "Add";
    return "Capture";
  }

  function positionPill(el) {
    // The root of an open capture is inert — hide the pill entirely.
    if (capture && el === capture.rootEl) {
      hidePill();
      return;
    }
    ensurePill();
    pill.textContent = pillLabel(el);
    const rect = el.getBoundingClientRect();
    pill.style.left = Math.max(rect.right - 96, rect.left) + "px";
    pill.style.top = Math.max(rect.top + 6, 0) + "px";
    pill.style.display = "block";
  }

  function hidePill() {
    if (pill) pill.style.display = "none";
    currentEl = null;
  }

  // --- Hover handling -----------------------------------------------------
  function handleMouseMove(e) {
    const el = document.elementFromPoint(e.clientX, e.clientY);

    if (pill && el && (el === pill || pill.contains(el))) {
      return; // cursor over the pill → keep it up, no flicker
    }

    const tweet = el ? el.closest('article[data-testid="tweet"], article') : null;
    if (tweet) {
      currentEl = tweet;
      positionPill(tweet);
    } else {
      hidePill();
    }
  }

  window.addEventListener("scroll", hidePill, { passive: true });
  document.addEventListener("mousemove", handleMouseMove, { passive: true });

  // Reset local capture state + highlights after a successful Save (the panel
  // triggers this via background).
  chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((msg) => {
    if (msg && msg.type === "capture-cleared") {
      if (capture) {
        if (capture.rootEl) capture.rootEl.removeAttribute(ADDED_ATTR);
        for (const el of capture.added.keys()) el.removeAttribute(ADDED_ATTR);
      }
      capture = null;
      hidePill();
    }
  });

  console.info("Capture content script ready.");
})();

// Side panel logic for Capture.
// Renders the in-progress capture as a live view: the root tweet plus any
// curated comments nested in an indented tree. Re-renders live when the
// content script adds/removes comments (via background broadcast). On Save it
// POSTs the whole recursive tree to the local server.

(() => {
  "use strict";

  const statusEl = document.getElementById("status");
  const contentEl = document.getElementById("content");
  const authorEl = document.getElementById("author");
  const urlEl = document.getElementById("tweet-url");
  const scrapeWarnEl = document.getElementById("scrape-warning");
  const textEl = document.getElementById("tweet-text");
  const linksEl = document.getElementById("links");
  const notesEl = document.getElementById("notes");
  const contextFieldset = document.getElementById("context-fieldset");
  const contextLegend = document.getElementById("context-legend");
  const contextTree = document.getElementById("context-tree");
  const saveBtn = document.getElementById("save");

  let serverUrl = "http://127.0.0.1:8765/api/capture";
  let current = null; // the root node of the in-progress capture (has children[])

  function setStatus(msg, kind) {
    statusEl.textContent = "";
    statusEl.className = "status " + (kind || "pending");
    statusEl.innerHTML = msg;
  }

  function clearStatus() {
    statusEl.textContent = "";
    statusEl.className = "";
  }

  function renderLinks(list) {
    linksEl.innerHTML = "";
    const arr = Array.isArray(list) ? list : [];
    if (arr.length === 0) {
      const li = document.createElement("li");
      li.className = "empty";
      li.textContent = "No links found.";
      linksEl.appendChild(li);
      return;
    }
    for (const link of arr) {
      const li = document.createElement("li");
      const cb = document.createElement("input");
      cb.type = "checkbox";
      cb.value = link;
      cb.checked = true;
      const label = document.createElement("label");
      label.textContent = link;
      li.appendChild(cb);
      li.appendChild(label);
      linksEl.appendChild(li);
    }
  }

  // Build one comment node row, indented by depth. Comment links are shown
  // plainly (captured automatically; no per-node toggles in v1).
  function nodeEl(node, depth) {
    const div = document.createElement("div");
    div.className = "cnode";
    div.style.paddingLeft = 6 + depth * 16 + "px";

    if (node.author) {
      const a = document.createElement("div");
      a.className = "c-author";
      a.textContent = node.author;
      div.appendChild(a);
    }
    const t = document.createElement("div");
    t.className = "c-text";
    t.textContent = node.tweet_text || "(no text scraped)";
    div.appendChild(t);
    if (node.tweet_url) {
      const u = document.createElement("span");
      u.className = "c-url";
      u.textContent = node.tweet_url;
      div.appendChild(u);
    }
    if (Array.isArray(node.links) && node.links.length) {
      const ul = document.createElement("ul");
      for (const link of node.links) {
        const li = document.createElement("li");
        li.textContent = link;
        ul.appendChild(li);
      }
      div.appendChild(ul);
    }
    return div;
  }

  function renderChildren(parentNode, depth, container) {
    for (const child of parentNode.children || []) {
      container.appendChild(nodeEl(child, depth));
      renderChildren(child, depth + 1, container);
    }
  }

  // Re-render the Context (comments tree) section only — called on live
  // updates so notes/text edits in the panel aren't clobbered.
  function renderContext() {
    const kids = (current && current.children) || [];
    contextFieldset.style.display = kids.length ? "block" : "none";
    contextLegend.textContent = "Context (" + kids.length + ")";
    contextTree.innerHTML = "";
    if (kids.length) renderChildren(current, 1, contextTree);
  }

  function renderRoot() {
    const node = current || {};
    authorEl.textContent = node.author || "Unknown author";
    urlEl.textContent = node.tweet_url || "";
    textEl.value = node.tweet_text || "";
    if (!node.tweet_text && !node.tweet_url) {
      scrapeWarnEl.style.display = "block";
    } else {
      scrapeWarnEl.style.display = "none";
    }
    renderLinks(node.links || []);
  }

  function render(data) {
    current = data || { tweet_url: "", author: "", tweet_text: "", links: [], parent_url: null, notes: "", children: [] };
    renderRoot();
    renderContext();
    notesEl.value = current.notes || "";
    contentEl.style.display = "block";
    clearStatus();
  }

  // Deep-clone current into a tree, apply panel edits (root text, notes,
  // checked root links), and return it for submission.
  function collectArtefact() {
    const tree = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(current || {}));
    tree.tweet_text = textEl.value.trim();
    tree.notes = notesEl.value.trim();
    const checkedLinks = [];
    for (const li of linksEl.querySelectorAll("li")) {
      const cb = li.querySelector('input[type="checkbox"]');
      if (cb && cb.checked && cb.value) checkedLinks.push(cb.value);
    }
    tree.links = checkedLinks;
    return tree;
  }

  async function submit() {
    if (!(current && current.tweet_url)) {
      setStatus("Nothing to save — no tweet URL.", "error");
      return;
    }
    const artefact = collectArtefact();
    saveBtn.disabled = true;
    setStatus("Saving…", "pending");
    try {
      const resp = await fetch(serverUrl, {
        method: "POST",
        headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
        body: JSON.stringify(artefact),
      });
      if (!resp.ok) throw new Error("HTTP " + resp.status);
      const body = await resp.json();
      setStatus(
        "Saved ✓ " + (body.captured_at ? "at " + body.captured_at : ""),
        "ok"
      );
      chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ type: "clear-pending-capture" });
      saveBtn.disabled = false;
    } catch (e) {
      setStatus(
        "<strong>Server offline</strong> — restart and try again. Your data is still here.",
        "error"
      );
      saveBtn.disabled = false;
    }
  }

  saveBtn.addEventListener("click", submit);

  // Live updates from the background (content added/removed a comment).
  chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((message) => {
    if (!message) return;
    if (message.type === "capture-updated") {
      chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ type: "get-pending-capture" }, (resp) => {
        if (chrome.runtime.lastError || !resp || !resp.data) return;
        current = resp.data;
        renderContext(); // refresh tree only; keep notes/text edits
      });
    } else if (message.type === "capture-cleared") {
      // Capture completed — reset the panel to the empty prompt.
      current = null;
      contentEl.style.display = "none";
      setStatus("Capture complete. Hover a tweet on X and click <strong>Capture</strong> to start again.", "ok");
    }
  });

  // Ask background for the pending capture (survives page navigation).
  chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ type: "get-pending-capture" }, (resp) => {
    if (chrome.runtime.lastError) {
      setStatus("Background unavailable. Reopen the panel.", "error");
      return;
    }
    if (resp && resp.serverUrl) serverUrl = resp.serverUrl;
    if (resp && resp.data) {
      render(resp.data);
    } else {
      contentEl.style.display = "none";
      setStatus("No pending capture. Hover a tweet on X and click <strong>Capture</strong>.", "pending");
    }
  });
})();

// Background service worker for Capture.
// Relays scraped data from content scripts to the side panel and keeps the
// pending capture state across page navigations. The panel survives
// navigation because state lives here (in chrome.storage.session), not in the
// page. The actual POST to the server is done by sidepanel.js.

const DEFAULT_PORT = 8765;
const SERVER_URL = `http://127.0.0.1:${DEFAULT_PORT}/api/capture`;

let pendingCapture = null; // kept in memory to survive navigation within a tab

// Open the side panel for the originating tab (or the active window).
async function openSidePanel(tabId) {
  try {
    if (tabId != null) {
      await chrome.sidePanel.open({ tabId });
    } else {
      const [tab] = await chrome.tabs.query({ active: true, currentWindow: true });
      if (tab && tab.id != null) await chrome.sidePanel.open({ tabId: tab.id });
    }
  } catch (e) {
    console.error("Capture: could not open side panel", e);
  }
}

chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener((message, sender, sendResponse) => {
  if (message.type === "capture-scraped") {
    pendingCapture = message.data;
    console.log("Capture: received capture-scraped, opening panel");
    openSidePanel(sender.tab && sender.tab.id);
    notifyPanelUpdated();
    sendResponse({ ok: true });
  } else if (message.type === "update-pending-capture") {
    // Live update while the panel is open — do not re-open the panel.
    pendingCapture = message.data;
    notifyPanelUpdated();
    sendResponse({ ok: true });
  } else if (message.type === "get-pending-capture") {
    sendResponse({ data: pendingCapture, serverUrl: SERVER_URL });
    return true; // async response
  } else if (message.type === "clear-pending-capture") {
    pendingCapture = null;
    notifyCaptureCleared();
    sendResponse({ ok: true });
  }
  return undefined;
});

// Tell an open side panel that the in-progress capture changed so it can
// re-render live (decision E). The panel ignores this if it isn't open.
function notifyPanelUpdated() {
  try {
    chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ type: "capture-updated" });
  } catch (e) {
    // no listener yet — fine
  }
}

// After a successful save, reset the content script (clear highlights and the
// open-capture state) and the panel (back to the empty prompt).
function notifyCaptureCleared() {
  try {
    chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ type: "capture-cleared" });
  } catch (e) {
    // no listeners — fine
  }
}

// Allow opening the panel from the toolbar action as well.
chrome.action.onClicked.addListener(async (tab) => {
  if (tab && tab.id != null) await openSidePanel(tab.id);
});

console.info("Capture background service worker ready.");

> pi can help you use the SDK. Ask it to build an integration for your use case.

# SDK

The SDK provides programmatic access to pi's agent capabilities. Use it to embed pi in other applications, build custom interfaces, or integrate with automated workflows.

**Example use cases:**
- Build a custom UI (web, desktop, mobile)
- Integrate agent capabilities into existing applications
- Create automated pipelines with agent reasoning
- Build custom tools that spawn sub-agents
- Test agent behavior programmatically

See [examples/sdk/](../examples/sdk/) for working examples from minimal to full control.

## Quick Start

```typescript
import { createAgentSession, ModelRuntime, SessionManager } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

const modelRuntime = await ModelRuntime.create();
const { session } = await createAgentSession({
  sessionManager: SessionManager.inMemory(),
  modelRuntime,
});

session.subscribe((event) => {
  if (event.type === "message_update" && event.assistantMessageEvent.type === "text_delta") {
    process.stdout.write(event.assistantMessageEvent.delta);
  }
});

await session.prompt("What files are in the current directory?");
```

## Installation

```bash
npm install @earendil-works/pi-coding-agent
```

The SDK is included in the main package. No separate installation needed.

## Core Concepts

### createAgentSession()

The main factory function for a single `AgentSession`.

`createAgentSession()` uses a `ResourceLoader` to supply extensions, skills, prompt templates, themes, and context files. If you do not provide one, it uses `DefaultResourceLoader` with standard discovery.

```typescript
import { createAgentSession, SessionManager } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

// Minimal: defaults with DefaultResourceLoader
const { session } = await createAgentSession();

// Custom: override specific options
const { session } = await createAgentSession({
  model: myModel,
  tools: ["read", "bash"],
  sessionManager: SessionManager.inMemory(),
});
```

### AgentSession

The session manages agent lifecycle, message history, model state, compaction, and event streaming.

```typescript
interface AgentSession {
  // Send a prompt and wait for completion
  prompt(text: string, options?: PromptOptions): Promise<void>;

  // Queue messages during streaming
  steer(text: string): Promise<void>;
  followUp(text: string): Promise<void>;

  // Subscribe to events (returns unsubscribe function)
  subscribe(listener: (event: AgentSessionEvent) => void): () => void;

  // Session info
  sessionFile: string | undefined;
  sessionId: string;

  // Model control
  setModel(model: Model): Promise<void>;
  setThinkingLevel(level: ThinkingLevel): void;
  cycleModel(): Promise<ModelCycleResult | undefined>;
  cycleThinkingLevel(): ThinkingLevel | undefined;

  // State access
  agent: Agent;
  model: Model | undefined;
  thinkingLevel: ThinkingLevel;
  messages: AgentMessage[];
  isStreaming: boolean;

  // In-place tree navigation within the current session file
  navigateTree(targetId: string, options?: { summarize?: boolean; customInstructions?: string; replaceInstructions?: boolean; label?: string }): Promise<{ editorText?: string; cancelled: boolean }>;

  // Compaction
  compact(customInstructions?: string): Promise<CompactionResult>;
  abortCompaction(): void;

  // Abort current operation
  abort(): Promise<void>;

  // Cleanup
  dispose(): void;
}
```

Session replacement APIs such as new-session, resume, fork, and import live on `AgentSessionRuntime`, not on `AgentSession`.

### createAgentSessionRuntime() and AgentSessionRuntime

Use the runtime API when you need to replace the active session and rebuild cwd-bound runtime state.
This is the same layer used by the built-in interactive, print, and RPC modes.

`createAgentSessionRuntime()` takes a runtime factory plus the initial cwd/session target. The factory closes over process-global fixed inputs, recreates cwd-bound services for the effective cwd, resolves session options against those services, and returns a full runtime result.

```typescript
import {
  type CreateAgentSessionRuntimeFactory,
  createAgentSessionFromServices,
  createAgentSessionRuntime,
  createAgentSessionServices,
  getAgentDir,
  SessionManager,
} from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

const createRuntime: CreateAgentSessionRuntimeFactory = async ({ cwd, sessionManager, sessionStartEvent }) => {
  const services = await createAgentSessionServices({ cwd });
  return {
    ...(await createAgentSessionFromServices({
      services,
      sessionManager,
      sessionStartEvent,
    })),
    services,
    diagnostics: services.diagnostics,
  };
};

const runtime = await createAgentSessionRuntime(createRuntime, {
  cwd: process.cwd(),
  agentDir: getAgentDir(),
  sessionManager: SessionManager.create(process.cwd()),
});
```

`AgentSessionRuntime` owns replacement of the active runtime across:

- `newSession()`
- `switchSession()`
- `fork()`
- clone flows via `fork(entryId, { position: "at" })`
- `importFromJsonl()`

Important behavior:

- `runtime.session` changes after those operations
- event subscriptions are attached to a specific `AgentSession`, so re-subscribe after replacement
- if you use extensions, call `runtime.session.bindExtensions(...)` again for the new session
- creation returns diagnostics on `runtime.diagnostics`
- if runtime creation or replacement fails, the method throws and the caller decides how to handle it

```typescript
let session = runtime.session;
let unsubscribe = session.subscribe(() => {});

await runtime.newSession();

unsubscribe();
session = runtime.session;
unsubscribe = session.subscribe(() => {});
```

### Prompting and Message Queueing

`PromptOptions` controls prompt expansion, queueing behavior while streaming, and prompt preflight notifications:

```typescript
interface PromptOptions {
  expandPromptTemplates?: boolean;
  images?: ImageContent[];
  streamingBehavior?: "steer" | "followUp";
  source?: InputSource;
  preflightResult?: (success: boolean) => void;
}
```

`preflightResult` is called once per `prompt()` invocation:

- `true` when the prompt was accepted, queued, or handled immediately
- `false` when prompt preflight rejected before acceptance

It fires before `prompt()` resolves. `prompt()` still resolves only after the full accepted run finishes, including retries. Failures after acceptance are reported through the normal event and message stream, not through `preflightResult(false)`.

The `prompt()` method handles prompt templates, extension commands, and message sending:

```typescript
// Basic prompt (when not streaming)
await session.prompt("What files are here?");

// With images
await session.prompt("What's in this image?", {
  images: [{ type: "image", source: { type: "base64", mediaType: "image/png", data: "..." } }]
});

// During streaming: must specify how to queue the message
await session.prompt("Stop and do this instead", { streamingBehavior: "steer" });
await session.prompt("After you're done, also check X", { streamingBehavior: "followUp" });
```

**Behavior:**
- **Extension commands** (e.g., `/mycommand`): Execute immediately, even during streaming. They manage their own LLM interaction via `pi.sendMessage()`.
- **File-based prompt templates** (from `.md` files): Expanded to their content before sending or queueing.
- **During streaming without `streamingBehavior`**: Throws an error. Use `steer()` or `followUp()` directly, or specify the option.
- **`preflightResult(true)`**: Means the prompt was accepted, queued, or handled immediately.
- **`preflightResult(false)`**: Means preflight rejected before acceptance.

For explicit queueing during streaming:

```typescript
// Queue a steering message for delivery after the current assistant turn finishes its tool calls
await session.steer("New instruction");

// Wait for agent to finish (delivered only when agent stops)
await session.followUp("After you're done, also do this");
```

Both `steer()` and `followUp()` expand file-based prompt templates but error on extension commands (extension commands cannot be queued).

### Agent and AgentState

The `Agent` class (from `@earendil-works/pi-agent-core`) handles the core LLM interaction. Access it via `session.agent`.

```typescript
// Access current state
const state = session.agent.state;

// state.messages: AgentMessage[] - conversation history
// state.model: Model - current model
// state.thinkingLevel: ThinkingLevel - current thinking level
// state.systemPrompt: string - system prompt
// state.tools: AgentTool[] - available tools
// state.streamingMessage?: AgentMessage - current partial assistant message
// state.errorMessage?: string - latest assistant error

// Replace messages (useful for branching or restoration)
session.agent.state.messages = messages; // copies the top-level array

// Replace tools
session.agent.state.tools = tools; // copies the top-level array

// Wait for agent to finish processing
await session.agent.waitForIdle();
```

### Events

Subscribe to events to receive streaming output and lifecycle notifications.

```typescript
session.subscribe((event) => {
  switch (event.type) {
    // Streaming text from assistant
    case "message_update":
      if (event.assistantMessageEvent.type === "text_delta") {
        process.stdout.write(event.assistantMessageEvent.delta);
      }
      if (event.assistantMessageEvent.type === "thinking_delta") {
        // Thinking output (if thinking enabled)
      }
      break;

    // Tool execution
    case "tool_execution_start":
      console.log(`Tool: ${event.toolName}`);
      break;
    case "tool_execution_update":
      // Streaming tool output
      break;
    case "tool_execution_end":
      console.log(`Result: ${event.isError ? "error" : "success"}`);
      break;

    // Message lifecycle
    case "message_start":
      // New message starting
      break;
    case "message_end":
      // Message complete
      break;

    // Agent lifecycle
    case "agent_start":
      // Agent started processing prompt
      break;
    case "agent_end":
      // Agent finished (event.messages contains new messages)
      break;

    // Turn lifecycle (one LLM response + tool calls)
    case "turn_start":
      break;
    case "turn_end":
      // event.message: assistant response
      // event.toolResults: tool results from this turn
      break;

    // Session events (queue, compaction, retry)
    case "queue_update":
      console.log(event.steering, event.followUp);
      break;
    case "compaction_start":
    case "compaction_end":
    case "auto_retry_start":
    case "auto_retry_end":
    case "summarization_retry_scheduled":
    case "summarization_retry_attempt_start":
    case "summarization_retry_finished":
      break;
  }
});
```

## Options Reference

### Directories

```typescript
const { session } = await createAgentSession({
  // Working directory for DefaultResourceLoader discovery
  cwd: process.cwd(), // default

  // Global config directory
  agentDir: "~/.pi/agent", // default (expands ~)
});
```

`cwd` is used by `DefaultResourceLoader` for:
- Project extensions (`.pi/extensions/`)
- Project skills:
  - `.pi/skills/`
  - `.agents/skills/` in `cwd` and ancestor directories (up to git repo root, or filesystem root when not in a repo)
- Project prompts (`.pi/prompts/`)
- Context files (`AGENTS.md` walking up from cwd)
- Session directory naming

`agentDir` is used by `DefaultResourceLoader` for:
- Global extensions (`extensions/`)
- Global skills:
  - `skills/` under `agentDir` (for example `~/.pi/agent/skills/`)
  - `~/.agents/skills/`
- Global prompts (`prompts/`)
- Global context file (`AGENTS.md`)
- Settings (`settings.json`)
- Custom models (`models.json`)
- Credentials (`auth.json`)
- Sessions (`sessions/`)

When you pass a custom `ResourceLoader`, `cwd` and `agentDir` no longer control resource discovery. They still influence session naming and tool path resolution.

### Model

```typescript
import { getModel } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai";
import { ModelRuntime } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

const modelRuntime = await ModelRuntime.create();

// Find specific built-in model (doesn't check if API key exists)
const opus = getModel("anthropic", "claude-opus-4-5");
if (!opus) throw new Error("Model not found");

// Find any model by provider/id, including custom models from models.json
// (doesn't check if API key exists)
const customModel = modelRuntime.getModel("my-provider", "my-model");

// Get only models that have valid authentication configured
const available = await modelRuntime.getAvailable();

const { session } = await createAgentSession({
  model: opus,
  thinkingLevel: "medium", // off, minimal, low, medium, high, xhigh, max

  // Models for cycling (Ctrl+P in interactive mode)
  scopedModels: [
    { model: opus, thinkingLevel: "high" },
    { model: haiku, thinkingLevel: "off" },
  ],

  modelRuntime,
});
```

If no model is provided:
1. Tries to restore from session (if continuing)
2. Uses default from settings
3. Falls back to first available model

To match CLI model parsing, use the exported resolver helpers:

```typescript
import {
  resolveCliModel,
  resolveModelScopeWithDiagnostics,
} from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

const cliModel = resolveCliModel({
  cliModel: "anthropic/claude-opus-4-5:high",
  modelRuntime,
});
if (cliModel.error) throw new Error(cliModel.error);
if (cliModel.warning) console.warn(cliModel.warning);

const { scopedModels, diagnostics } = await resolveModelScopeWithDiagnostics(
  ["anthropic/*:high", "gpt-5"],
  modelRuntime,
);
for (const diagnostic of diagnostics) {
  console.warn(diagnostic.message);
}
```

`resolveCliModel()` uses all registered models so `--api-key` style first-time setup can resolve a model before stored auth exists. `resolveModelScopeWithDiagnostics()` matches `--models` and `enabledModels` semantics while returning warnings instead of printing them.

> See [examples/sdk/02-custom-model.ts](../examples/sdk/02-custom-model.ts)

### API Keys and OAuth

Authentication resolution priority (handled by `ModelRuntime`):
1. Runtime overrides (via `setRuntimeApiKey`, not persisted)
2. Stored credentials in `auth.json` (API keys or OAuth tokens)
3. Environment variables (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `OPENAI_API_KEY`, etc.)
4. Fallback resolver (for custom provider keys from `models.json`)

```typescript
import { InMemoryCredentialStore } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai";
import { createAgentSession, ModelRuntime } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

// Default: uses ~/.pi/agent/auth.json and ~/.pi/agent/models.json
const modelRuntime = await ModelRuntime.create();

// Provider-owned auth methods and current status
for (const provider of modelRuntime.getProviders()) {
  const status = await modelRuntime.checkAuth(provider.id);
  console.log(provider.name, provider.auth, status);
}

// Runtime API key override (not persisted to disk)
modelRuntime.setRuntimeApiKey("anthropic", "sk-my-temp-key");

// Custom credential and model locations
const customRuntime = await ModelRuntime.create({
  authPath: "/my/app/auth.json",
  modelsPath: "/my/app/models.json",
});

// Or inject any pi-ai CredentialStore
const credentials = new InMemoryCredentialStore();
const inMemoryRuntime = await ModelRuntime.create({ credentials });

const { session } = await createAgentSession({
  modelRuntime: customRuntime,
});
```

> See [examples/sdk/09-api-keys-and-oauth.ts](../examples/sdk/09-api-keys-and-oauth.ts)

### System Prompt

Use a `ResourceLoader` to override the system prompt:

```typescript
import { createAgentSession, DefaultResourceLoader } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

const loader = new DefaultResourceLoader({
  systemPromptOverride: () => "You are a helpful assistant.",
});
await loader.reload();

const { session } = await createAgentSession({ resourceLoader: loader });
```

> See [examples/sdk/03-custom-prompt.ts](../examples/sdk/03-custom-prompt.ts)

### Tools

Specify which built-in tools to enable:

- Built-in tool names: `read`, `bash`, `edit`, `write`, `grep`, `find`, `ls`
- Default built-ins: `read`, `bash`, `edit`, `write`
- `noTools: "all"` disables all tools
- `noTools: "builtin"` disables default built-ins while keeping extension and custom tools enabled
- `excludeTools` disables specific built-in, extension, or custom tool names after any `tools` allowlist is applied

The `edit` tool returns `details.diff` for Pi's TUI display and `details.patch` as a standard unified patch for SDK consumers.

```typescript
import { createAgentSession } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

// Read-only mode
const { session } = await createAgentSession({
  tools: ["read", "grep", "find", "ls"],
});

// Pick specific tools
const { session } = await createAgentSession({
  tools: ["read", "bash", "grep"],
});

// Disable one tool while keeping the rest available
const { session } = await createAgentSession({
  excludeTools: ["ask_question"],
});
```

#### Tools with Custom cwd

When you pass a custom `cwd`, `createAgentSession()` builds selected built-in tools for that cwd.

```typescript
import { createAgentSession, SessionManager } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

const cwd = "/path/to/project";

// Use default tools for custom cwd
const { session } = await createAgentSession({
  cwd,
  sessionManager: SessionManager.inMemory(cwd),
});

// Or pick specific tools for custom cwd
const { session } = await createAgentSession({
  cwd,
  tools: ["read", "bash", "grep"],
  sessionManager: SessionManager.inMemory(cwd),
});
```

> See [examples/sdk/05-tools.ts](../examples/sdk/05-tools.ts)

### Custom Tools

```typescript
import { Type } from "typebox";
import { createAgentSession, defineTool } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

// Inline custom tool
const myTool = defineTool({
  name: "my_tool",
  label: "My Tool",
  description: "Does something useful",
  parameters: Type.Object({
    input: Type.String({ description: "Input value" }),
  }),
  execute: async (_toolCallId, params) => ({
    content: [{ type: "text", text: `Result: ${params.input}` }],
    details: {},
  }),
});

// Pass custom tools directly
const { session } = await createAgentSession({
  customTools: [myTool],
});
```

Use `defineTool()` for standalone definitions and arrays like `customTools: [myTool]`. Inline `pi.registerTool({ ... })` already infers parameter types correctly.

Custom tools passed via `customTools` are combined with extension-registered tools. Extensions loaded by the ResourceLoader can also register tools via `pi.registerTool()`.

If you pass `tools`, include each custom or extension tool name you want enabled, for example `tools: ["read", "bash", "my_tool"]`.

> See [examples/sdk/05-tools.ts](../examples/sdk/05-tools.ts)

### Extensions

Extensions are loaded by the `ResourceLoader`. `DefaultResourceLoader` discovers extensions from `~/.pi/agent/extensions/`, `.pi/extensions/`, and settings.json extension sources.

```typescript
import { createAgentSession, DefaultResourceLoader } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

const loader = new DefaultResourceLoader({
  additionalExtensionPaths: ["/path/to/my-extension.ts"],
  extensionFactories: [
    (pi) => {
      pi.on("agent_start", () => {
        console.log("[Inline Extension] Agent starting");
      });
    },
  ],
});
await loader.reload();

const { session } = await createAgentSession({ resourceLoader: loader });
```

Extensions can register tools, subscribe to events, add commands, and more. See [extensions.md](extensions.md) for the full API.

**Named inline extensions:** By default, inline factories display as `<inline:1>`, `<inline:2>`, etc. in the startup Extensions list. To show a descriptive name instead, wrap the factory:

```typescript
import type { InlineExtension } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

const myProvider: InlineExtension = {
  name: "my-provider",
  factory: (pi) => {
    pi.on("agent_start", () => {
      console.log("[my-provider] Agent starting");
    });
  },
};

const loader = new DefaultResourceLoader({
  extensionFactories: [myProvider],
});
```

This displays as `<inline:my-provider>` instead of `<inline:1>`. Bare factory functions are still accepted for backward compatibility.

**Event Bus:** Extensions can communicate via `pi.events`. Pass a shared `eventBus` to `DefaultResourceLoader` if you need to emit or listen from outside:

```typescript
import { createEventBus, DefaultResourceLoader } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

const eventBus = createEventBus();
const loader = new DefaultResourceLoader({
  eventBus,
});
await loader.reload();

eventBus.on("my-extension:status", (data) => console.log(data));
```

> See [examples/sdk/06-extensions.ts](../examples/sdk/06-extensions.ts) and [docs/extensions.md](extensions.md)

